{"records":[{"id":"2rrn8ngvsz","title":"Tumor-Associated Schwann Cell Remodeling under Metabolic Stress via Lactate Sensing Orchestrates","description":"Diabetes mellitus (DM) is a known risk factor for pancreatic cancer, but the underlying mechanisms remain elusive. Using in vivo models of diabetes-driven pancreatic tumorigenesis and metabolomics analysis, we demonstrate that metabolic stress induced by DM promotes pancreatic cancer progression by remodeling tumor-associated Schwann cells (TASs), impairing CD8+ T cell function. We discover that Mettl16+ Cd276+ Nectin2+ Schwann cells play a crucial role in mediating the pro-carcinogenic effects of lactate. Remodeled METTL16+ Schwann cells secret immune evasion factors (CD276, NECTIN2), contributing to anti-PD1 resistance. Furthermore, rosuvastatin was identified in this study as a novel METTL16 inhibitor for pancreatic cancer under metabolic stress. In conclusion, our study underscores the critical role of lactate in the metabolic reprogramming of pancreatic cancer mediated by Schwann cell remodeling, revealing novel pathways for therapeutic intervention.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-10-01T10:24:44.344Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/2rrn8ngvsz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/2rrn8ngvsz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Baiyong","last_name":"Shen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Tumor","Metabolism"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"97p7knbk42","title":"Pancreatic Cancer Biomedical Knowledge Graph","description":"This dataset comprises approximately 1 million high-confidence biomedical triples focused on pancreatic cancer, constructed from a curated set of 23 relevant biomedical entities (KRAS, TP53, gemcitabine) and 11 common relation types ( mutated_in, treats, interacts_with). Each triple is embedded in a synthetic, natural language sentence mimicking scientific phrasing and is paired with a simulated attention score ranging from 0.75 to 1.00, reflecting transformer-based model confidence. Heuristic boosting was applied to biologically plausible combinations, resulting in an average attention score near 0.88. This structured resource is ideal for training, validating, or benchmarking biomedical NLP models and knowledge extraction systems within the context of pancreatic cancer.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-05-23T07:39:46.515Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/97p7knbk42","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/97p7knbk42"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Murari","last_name":"Ambati","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Pancreatic Cancer"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"32ncbgtw59","title":"Tumor-Associated Schwann Cell Remodeling under Metabolic Stress via Lactate Sensing Orchestrates","description":"Diabetes mellitus (DM) is a known risk factor for pancreatic cancer, but the underlying mechanisms remain elusive. Using in vivo models of diabetes-driven pancreatic tumorigenesis and metabolomics analysis, we demonstrate that metabolic stress induced by DM promotes pancreatic cancer progression by remodeling tumor-associated Schwann cells (TASs), impairing CD8+ T cell function. We discover that Mettl16+ Cd276+ Nectin2+ Schwann cells play a crucial role in mediating the pro-carcinogenic effects of lactate. Remodeled METTL16+ Schwann cells secret immune evasion factors (CD276, NECTIN2), contributing to anti-PD1 resistance. Furthermore, rosuvastatin was identified in this study as a novel METTL16 inhibitor for pancreatic cancer under metabolic stress. In conclusion, our study underscores the critical role of lactate in the metabolic reprogramming of pancreatic cancer mediated by Schwann cell remodeling, revealing novel pathways for therapeutic intervention.\nBiotin pull-down assay\nThe Magic Dynabeads MyOne Streptavidin T1 were pre-incubated with free biotin or biotinylated lactate at room temperature for 30 minutes to saturate the bead surface with biotin. Subsequently, the pre-treated beads were mixed with the cell lysate of interest and incubated overnight at 4°C with rotation. The beads were then washed four times with PBS buffer to remove non-specifically bound molecules. After washing, the proteins were separated from the beads, and the presence and expression levels of the target protein were detected using techniques such as Western blotting or proteomics.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-10-10T08:17:48.997Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/32ncbgtw59","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/32ncbgtw59"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Baiyong","last_name":"Shen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Proteomics","Tumor","Metabolism"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"cg2w69gpdf","title":"Machine Learning-Optimized Metabolic Biomarker Panel for Precision Screening of Early-Stage Pancreatic Cancer in New-Onset Diabetes","description":"Machine Learning-Optimized Metabolic Biomarker Panel for Precision Screening of Early-Stage Pancreatic Cancer in New-Onset DiabetesMachine Learning-Optimized Metabolic Biomarker Panel for PrecisionMachine Learning-Optimized Metabolic Biomarker Panel for Precision Screening of Early-Stage Pancreatic Cancer in New-Onset Diabetes Screening of Early-Stage Pancreatic Cancer in New-Machine Learning-Optimized Metabolic Biomarker Panel for Precision Screening of Early-Stage Pancreatic Cancer in New-Onset DiabetesOnset DiabetesMachine Learning-Optimized Metabolic Biomarker Panel for Precision Screening of Early-Stage Pancreatic Cancer in New-Onset Diabetes","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-11-25T09:47:07.381Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/cg2w69gpdf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/cg2w69gpdf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Zhiyuan","last_name":"Cheng","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer Diagnosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"y2pmnjrnyr","title":"PRRX2-Mediated Transcriptional Regulation of GLI2 Promotes Cell Proliferation, Invasion and Metastasis by Suppressing Cellular Senescence via Hedgehog Signaling in Pancreatic Cancer","description":"Our discovery of the role of PRRX2 in pancreatic cancer and its mechanism provides new ideas for the diagnosis and treatment of pancreatic cancer.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-05-13T07:28:46.215Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/y2pmnjrnyr","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/y2pmnjrnyr"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"wei","last_name":"huang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Pancreatic Cancer"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"wcb2xsg6pj","title":"Heterogeneous effects of calorie content and nutritional components underlie dietary influence on pancreatic cancer susceptibility in mice and humans","description":"Pancreatic cancer is a rare but fatal form of cancer, the fourth highest in absolute mortality. Known risk factors include obesity, diet and type 2 diabetes, however the low incidence rate and interconnection of these factors confound the isolation of individual effects. Here we use epidemiological analysis of prospective human cohorts and parallel tracking of pancreatic cancer in mice to dissect the impacts of obesity, diet and diabetes on pancreatic cancer. Through longitudinal monitoring and multi-omics analysis in mice we found distinct effects of protein, sugar and fat dietary components, with dietary sugars increasing Mad2l1 expression and tumor proliferation. Using epidemiological approaches in humans, we dietary sugars gave a MAD2L1 genotype-dependent increased susceptibility to pancreatic cancer. Translation of these results to a clinical setting could aid identification of the at-risk population for screening and potential harness dietary modification as a therapeutic measure. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-06-25T16:28:00.196Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/wcb2xsg6pj","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/wcb2xsg6pj"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"James","last_name":"Dooley","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Vasiliki","last_name":"Lagou","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Adrian","last_name":"Liston","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Genetics","Cancer","Nutrition","Pancreatic Cancer","Epidemiology of Pancreatic Cancer"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"n56g9935y8","title":"Arginine Methylation of MST2 by PRMT5 Inhibits Hippo Signaling and Promotes the Progression of Pancreatic Cancer","description":"As a classic tumor suppressor pathway, Hippo signaling axis is activated by various extra-pathway factors to regulate cell differentiation and organ development. However, recent studies have reported that the activation of Hippo signaling pathway may be more dependent on the autophosphorylation of its core kinase cassette. Here, we demonstrate that protein arginine methyltransferase 5 (PRMT5) is involved in inducing the inactivation of Hippo signaling pathway in pancreatic cancer. Our study shows that the initiator serine/threonine kinase 3 (STK3, also known as MST2) of Hippo signaling pathway can be symmetrically di-methylated at arginine-461 (R461) and arginine-467 (R467) in the SARAH domain by PRMT5, and the methylated MST2 suppresses its autophosphorylation and kinase activity by blocking the formation of homodimer, thereby inactivating Hippo signaling pathway in pancreatic cancer. Moreover, we also discover that the specific PRMT5 inhibitor GSK3326595 re-activates the dysregulated Hippo signaling pathway and inhibits the growth of human-derived pancreatic cancer xenografts in immunodeficient mice, which provides a theoretical foundation for the clinical application of PRMT5 inhibitor in pancreatic cancer.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-05-12T06:29:15.301Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/n56g9935y8","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/n56g9935y8"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"sunyan","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"202203021211042"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"82102789"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"82073321"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"82073178"}],"categories":["Pancreatic Cancer","Hippo Signaling Pathway","Molecular Target"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ybmr387wyv","title":"Aberrant accumulation of Kras-dependent enhancer RNAs during tumor progression renders cancer cells susceptible to PAF1 depletion","description":"KRAS is the most commonly mutated oncogene in human cancer and mutant KRAS is responsible for over 90% of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC), the most lethal cancer. Here, we show that RNA polymerase II associated factor 1 complex (PAF1C) is specifically required for the survival of PDAC but not normal adult pancreatic cells. We show that PAF1C maintains cancer cell genomic stability by restraining the over accumulation of enhancer RNAs (eRNAs) and PROMPTs driven by mutant Kras. Loss of PAF1C leads to cancer-specific lengthening and accumulation of pervasive transcripts on chromatin, and concomitant aberrant R-loop formation and DNA damage, which in turn trigger cell death. We go on to demonstrate that the global transcriptional hyperactivation driven by Kras signaling during tumorigenesis underlies the specific demand for PAF1C by cancer cells. Our work provides insights in how enhancer transcription hyperactivation causes general transcription factor addiction during tumorigenesis.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-08-09T11:01:29.655Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ybmr387wyv","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ybmr387wyv"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mo","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xinhong","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xiangzheng","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yingxue","last_name":"Du","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Di","last_name":"Zou","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chen","last_name":"Tian","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yong","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xun","last_name":"Lan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Charles","last_name":"David","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Qianwen","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/027s68j25","name":"Ministry of Science and Technology of the People's Republic of China","grant_id":"2020YFA0509402"},{"id":null,"name":"Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"92068114"},{"id":"https://ror.org/027s68j25","name":"Ministry of Science and Technology of the People's Republic of China","grant_id":"2021YFA1300100"}],"categories":["Cell Biology","Tumor Biology","Pancreatic Cancer"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"3c954wd6k6","title":"Beta-Cell Adaptation Unveiled: The Role of Myokines in Insulin Resistant mice. Related material.","description":"Insulin resistance necessitates pancreatic beta cells adaptation to maintain glycemia. Beta-cell mass adaptation can occur via beta-cell proliferation or via beta-cell neogenesis (de novo beta-cell production). To clarify the specific contribution and mechanisms of beta-cell adaptation in response to insulin resistance; we took advantage of corticosterone-induced insulin resistance in young and old mice. We used quantitative 3D analysis of cleared whole pancreas immunostained for insulin. The bioinformatic pipeline and original code used for the 3D analysis is provided in the file \"LibozBeaupere2024-MATLABscripts.zip\". We find that insulin resistant myotubes can secrete factors that induce beta-cell differentiation, recapitulating in vitro a multi-organs crosstalk. Using a combination of RNA sequencing (Illumina, Novaseq6000, 150bp paired-end) and functional analysis on a pancreatic differentiation model, we demonstrate that 3 myokines stimulate beta-cell differentiation in vitro. RNAseq analyzed data (differentially expressed genes) from 3 independent biological replicates of corticosterone- or VEH-treated myotubes are provided in the file \"Table2-DEG.xlsx\", along with sequencing quality check \"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-08-12T15:45:52.432Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/3c954wd6k6","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/3c954wd6k6"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Alexandrine","last_name":"Liboz","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Carine","last_name":"Beaupere","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jean-Yves","last_name":"Tinevez","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ghislaine","last_name":"Guillemain","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Bertrand","last_name":"Blondeau","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/04w6kn183","name":"Fondation pour la Recherche Médicale","grant_id":"EQU201903007868"},{"id":"https://ror.org/04w6kn183","name":"Fondation pour la Recherche Médicale","grant_id":"EQU202103012732"},{"id":"https://ror.org/05gdhxj91","name":"Société Francophone du Diabète","grant_id":null},{"id":"https://ror.org/02vjkv261","name":"Inserm","grant_id":null}],"categories":["3D Imaging","RNA Sequencing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"v475rd4282","title":"Prognostic Prediction and Investigation of Underlying Immune Mechanisms in Pancreatic Adenocarcinoma Based on Immunogenic Death-Associated lncRNA Expression：Dataset and microscopic images","description":"We accessed the GeneCards database and identified 177 genes associated with ICD, with a score greater than 35. Data from TCGA , including transcriptome expression profiles, single nucleotide mutation data, clinical characteristic data, and survival information for patients diagnosed with pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PAAD), were retrieved. Among the 185 samples, 181 were tumor tissues, and 4 were normal tissues. After excluding 3 samples lacking survival information, the remaining samples were randomly assigned to the training and testing sets. Based on the selected immunogenic cell death-related lncRNAs, we constructed a prognostic model for pancreatic cancer. Additionally, we verified the effect of LINC00705 on pancreatic cancer cells PANC-1 and MiaPaca-2 through in vitro cell experiments. The results showed that LINC00705 significantly enhanced the proliferation, migration, and invasion abilities of pancreatic cancer cells PANC-1 and MiaPaca-2, providing a novel biomarker for the prediction of pancreatic cancer prognosis.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-01-17T19:32:09.066Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/v475rd4282","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/v475rd4282"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Hao","last_name":"Zhu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Long Noncoding RNA"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":130820,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":150,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":130820,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":130253,"restricted":64,"embargoed":503},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2254},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":656},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":478},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":432},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":423},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"Conselho Nacional de Desenvolvimento Científico e Tecnológico","count":405},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":380},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":325},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":270},{"id":"https://ror.org/00k4n6c32","name":"European Commission","count":225}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":128192},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1903},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":183},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":135},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":76},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":57},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":40},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":31},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":512},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":406},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":361},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":338},{"id":"48c8fa0f-965c-5cbf-a859-a5363432478b","name":"Universidade de Sao Paulo","count":301}]}}